From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rocket: wait for a running IRQ handler before resetting a
 core

rocket_reset() calls drm_sched_stop(), which stops the scheduler and
returns.  It does not wait for a threaded handler that is already
running, so the comment that followed it -- "Remaining interrupts have
been handled" -- stated an assumption rather than something the code
arranged.  A completion handler that is mid-flight when the reset starts
keeps running against a core the reset is about to put through
rocket_core_reset(), and against an in_flight_job the reset has already
dropped.

Call synchronize_irq(core->irq) after drm_sched_stop() and reword the
comment to say what holds afterwards.

Mask the block first.  rocket_job_hw_submit() arms a completion pair in
INTERRUPT_MASK on every submit and only the hardirq clears it, so on an
ordinary timeout the mask is still live and a completion can arrive
after the sync returns.  Waiting without masking fences the handler that
is running, not the one the hardware is still entitled to raise.  The
next submit re-arms the mask, so nothing is lost by clearing it here.

That write is guarded by pm_runtime_get_if_active(), and it needs to be.
It is the first register access rocket_reset() makes, and the function
holds no runtime PM reference of its own: the only one in the window
belongs to in_flight_job, and the completion path can have put it and
cleared the pointer before the timeout worker arrives.  drm_sched_stop()
sits in between, can block, and subtracts every pending job's credits,
so rocket_job_is_idle() is true and nothing keeps the core resumed.  A
register access with the domain down takes an async SError on this
hardware, so the reset must not be what causes one.

It has to go before the scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) rather than
inside it.  rocket_job_handle_irq() takes job_lock -- 090 moved the
register writes under it, so it now holds that lock for the whole
handler -- and waiting for the handler while holding the lock it wants
would deadlock instead of fencing anything.  Nothing is held at that
point, and both callers, rocket_job_timedout() and rocket_reset_work(),
run in process context, so sleeping there is allowed.

This does not stop a handler that has already read in_flight_job from
finishing its work on the job the reset is about to drop.  That window
needs the check and the register writes to be one step under the lock,
which is what 090 does; the two are complementary.

From the v8 posting of the RK3576 series -- Jiaxing Hu's patch, suggested
by Igor Paunovic -- carried here because the function is the RK3588's own
and the fix is not RK3576-specific.

  https://lore.kernel.org/linux-rockchip/20260817113603.1436067-3-gahing@gahingwoo.com/

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 47f1e4c70..638019b90 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -467,9 +467,41 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 	drm_sched_stop(&core->sched, bad);
 
 	/*
-	 * Remaining interrupts have been handled, but we might still have
-	 * stuck jobs. Let's make sure the PM counters stay balanced by
-	 * manually calling pm_runtime_put_noidle().
+	 * Mask the block before waiting.  rocket_job_hw_submit() arms a
+	 * completion pair in INTERRUPT_MASK on every submit and only the
+	 * hardirq clears it, so on an ordinary timeout it is still live and a
+	 * completion can arrive after the sync below returns.  The next submit
+	 * re-arms it, so nothing is lost here.
+	 *
+	 * Only when the device is already awake, though.  This function holds
+	 * no runtime PM reference of its own: the only one in the window
+	 * belongs to in_flight_job, and the completion path may have put it
+	 * and cleared the pointer before the timeout worker got here.
+	 * drm_sched_stop() above can block for a long time, and it drops
+	 * every pending job's credits, so rocket_job_is_idle() is true and
+	 * nothing keeps the core resumed.  On this hardware a register access
+	 * with the domain down takes an async SError, so a reset must not be
+	 * the thing that causes one.
+	 */
+	if (pm_runtime_get_if_active(core->dev) > 0) {
+		rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_MASK, 0x0);
+		pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(core->dev);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * drm_sched_stop() returns without waiting for a threaded handler that
+	 * is already running, so wait for one here.  This has to stay outside
+	 * job_lock: rocket_job_handle_irq() takes that lock, so waiting for the
+	 * handler while holding it would be waiting for a handler that is
+	 * waiting for us.  Nothing is held at this point, and both callers run
+	 * in process context, so sleeping here is allowed.
+	 */
+	synchronize_irq(core->irq);
+
+	/*
+	 * No handler is running now, but we might still have stuck jobs. Let's
+	 * make sure the PM counters stay balanced by manually calling
+	 * pm_runtime_put_noidle().
 	 */
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
 		if (core->in_flight_job)
